===== books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-17.md =====
     1	# Chapter 17 — Priced Too Well
     2	
     3	Lowmarch had gone to the first cold while they were east of it, and he catalogued the difference from the road's last rise, because he had catalogued the town from that rise three times now and wanted the fourth to be honest.
     4	
     5	The river lower and darker. The ferry landing with half the bales it had carried in the autumn, and those under canvas. The meadow where the five of them had slept in the first weeks, empty, the grass gone to straw, two tent-rings in it where crews had been and were not. Smoke from every roof that had a chimney and from three that had only a hole. The board's room at the lower inn with its landing door shut against the wind and its road door open a hand's width for the smoke, and through the hand's width, a plank on two barrels, and a man at it with one arm, writing.
     6	
     7	Five of them, on the hundred and twelfth day past the Line, with a mule and three boxes and a girl with her arm across her chest and a taped right foot she put down flat at every stride, coming down into a town that had priced them at the bottom of a wall a season ago and had been moving them up it a line at a time for their outcomes, which was the only honest way to move.
     8	
     9	Oryn was on her route. She had gone out of Thornwater's gate at first light on the hundred and eleventh with the leather roll on her back and the mule's spare rope in her hand for the next hold's beast, north, on a road that bent as every road out here bent, and had not looked back, because a healer walking her circuit does not look back at a crew walking off it, and the crew had not needed her to. He had the seven stops in the order she had said them at a table, and the one after Thornwater was a ford called Millrace, on the Stair road, a day south of Lowmarch; by her count she would be at its table by tomorrow's dusk, and he had not said so to anyone, because nobody had asked and the count was the same for everyone.
    10	
    11	---
    12	
    13	Pike said the Fallow Ring's line before they had the door shut.
    14	
    15	He said it to the book, in the voice he used for a price, without looking up, because the procedure was that the line was read aloud before and after entry, and he had entered it eleven days ago off a carter's word from Thornwater, and this was the after. "*Recovery, Fallow road, broker's posting. Taken. Witnessed. Completed: incomplete — instruments not recovered — crew returned. Six went in. Six came out. No price paid.*" The pen stopped. "That's said."
    16	
    17	"That's said," Cael said, because somebody from the crew had to, and it had always been him.
    18	
    19	"Costs you a line." Pike put two fingers flat on the ledger and turned it, with one push, so that it faced across the plank, which he had done once before and Cael had never forgotten the feel of a page turned toward him. Eight completed lines under the five names. The eighth said what he had just heard it say. "Incomplete's incomplete. Instruments not recovered's not recovered. The wall goes by outcome, and the outcome was you went and came back with nothing to show for a hundred marks, and I've moved you down for it, because if I don't move a crew down for that I've no business moving one up for the other thing." He took the fingers off the ledger. "Eleventh. Teague's ten up."
    20	
    21	Nobody argued. It was arithmetic, and it was correct, and Cael had spent four years learning that a ledger that could not move you down was not a ledger.
    22	
    23	"And," Pike said.
    24	
    25	He did not say it to the book. He said it across the plank, to the five of them, in the same voice, and the same voice made it a different thing.
    26	
    27	"I've kept this wall thirty years. Thirteen crews on it now, and I've had forty-odd across the years, and I've walked past the Fallow Ring two hundred times and never once stepped over its line, and I've never once had a crew on my sheet that did. Not once. Not twice, which is the number the road counts." He looked at the eighth line where it lay. "You went in. You came out. Six in and six out, and the sixth a healer, and the only thing in my book that's got room for that is the outcome column, and the outcome column says *incomplete*." He picked the pen up. "The wall's the wall. I've moved you down. I'm telling you what the wall can't say, because I keep it and I'd sooner you heard it from the man who keeps it than worked it out from the room."
    28	
    29	The room had worked it out.
    30	
    31	He catalogued it, because it was the first room in a season that had had anything to work out about them. The board's room at the lower inn at the end of the day, a dozen people in it under a low roof, crews and carters and the hide-woman with her ledger, and every one of them had gone quiet at Pike's *six went in* and stayed quiet through *six came out* and was now not looking at the five of them in the way a room does not look at a thing it has decided about. Not fear. He had had fear from rooms for four years and knew its weight. This was the thing the Concourse had done on the bluff when a name went up a board it should not have been on: the room updating, all at once, without a word, on an outcome it had watched happen and could not price.
    32	
    33	He had the room's pieces, because he had catalogued them coming in: the hide-woman at the middle table with her ledger shut on her thumb, who had looked at the five of them in the first week as at a thing that would be gone by the snow; the crew of three from the autumn, new canvas gone grey, at the table by the road door; two carters at the fire with their backs to it, one of whom had walked the Thornwater road behind them in the first week and said *first crew I've hired that knew to shut up*; the boy who carried the ledger up from the landing when the light went off the water. Every one of them had heard *six went in*. Not one of them said anything, and the not-saying was the room's whole verdict, and it was a better one than the wall could give, because the wall had a column for it and the room did not.
    34	
    35	Teague stood up.
    36	
    37	He was at the best table with his three, and he stood up when the five of them came in off the road and did not sit down again until they had crossed to the plank, and it was not a greeting. He did not nod. He did not speak. He stood as a captain stands when the ledger is about to change and he means the room to see that he saw it first — the weight down through the heels, the shoulders low and square, the hands loose — and when Pike had said *eleventh* he sat down, and turned his shoulder to the fire, and that was the whole of it.
    38	
    39	Cael filed it beside the eighth line, and found that the two of them together said more than either.
    40	
    41	Pike rewrote the wall that evening, in full, in the one-armed hand, as he rewrote it every time a line moved: thirteen crews on one sheet, top to bottom, and the five names eleventh, one below where they had been, with the mule-named crew still thirteenth and a crew of three from the autumn twelfth, and Teague's four at the top where they had been for three years. He pinned it where it went, by the road door, and stood back from it the way he stood back from a price he had just said, and did not look at the five of them, because the sheet was the saying and the saying was for the room.
    42	
    43	---
    44	
    45	The posting was on the wall between the door and the plank, where Pike put the ones that had been read and returned by everyone.
    46	
    47	He saw the seal before he saw the paper. Dark wax, a hook and a bar, in the corner — the landing broker's, the one on a third of the bales — and thin broker's stock under it, and a clerk's hand, even, schooled, the letters sitting on a line that had not been drawn. He read it from the doorway, upside down, and then took it off its nail and read it the right way up at the plank, and Karis read it over his shoulder, and Seln did not read it at all, because Seln had read it from the doorway with the rest of the wall and had already gone still in the way he went still at a shelf.
    48	
    49	*Recovery. Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Stair road. South of Lowmarch; the first day; where the road drops off the ridge and the ground opens. Three hundred marks (three hundred).*
    50	
    51	Then the seal, and nothing else. No crew's mark. No HOLD. No name beside a word.
    52	
    53	"Same broker," Pike said, to the book. He had not been asked. "Same hand on it. Same kind of paper. Posted the day after the ferry went out — the day after your report went west, if you're counting, and I've watched that boy count since the first week, so I'd say he is." The pen went on. "That broker's had bales on my landing five years and never once posted a slip until the Fallow one. Now two. Nothing else in a year. Nothing else ever." He looked up, for the first time since they had come in. "I'd not take it. I said so to Teague when he read it, and he said he'd worked that out for himself, and put it back, and it's been on that nail nine days."
    54	
    55	Three hundred. The Fallow one had been a hundred, dry, and had been the best number on the board by forty. This was three times that, for a site one day south instead of four days east, and it hung on a nail where every crew on the wall had read it and put it back.
    56	
    57	Karis had her finger on *the Stair road*.
    58	
    59	"That's the carter's second circle," she said. Her voice was nearly whole; it caught on *circle* and came back. "South, a day, where the road drops. It's on the map with a blank round it. It's a site."
    60	
    61	"It's a site," Seln said.
    62	
    63	He said it to the plank, and then he took the slip out of Cael's hand, gently, as he had once taken a case off a shelf, and laid it flat on the wood under the river stone, and put one finger on it, and gave them the chapter he had been carrying since the doorway.
    64	
    65	---
    66	
    67	"Registry prose." The finger on *abandoned at a site*. "Same as the first. *Site*, not *the Stair*; *abandoned*, not *left*; the price twice, figures and words. A clerk inside wrote this. The same clerk. I could put the two slips against each other and show you the hand, and Pike's already done it, because Pike reads paper."
    68	
