    56	
    57	They came over the saddle above Lowmarch in the afternoon of the hundred and twelfth day, and he stopped on the crown of it and counted what was there.
    58	
    59	It was the fourth time that he had looked down on that town from high ground. He had learned on the first that it was a river, and a ferry, and two inns, and a smith, and a meadow with no wall. On the others he had learned to read the meadow as he had once read a board, since it changed with every week, and whatever was true of that country showed there first.
    60	
    61	The cold had been at it. Where forty fires had burned when the roads were dry, he made out twelve, drawn in close about the great ash, and half the trodden grass had gone back to white. The ferry's cable stood across the water like a chalk line, with frost on it. The scow lay on the near bank. A brown gelding with one white foot was walking his circle in the capstan-shed's doorway with a blanket on his back, and Cael was glad to see that somebody had thought of the blanket. Both inns were smoking. There was a skin of ice along the eastern bank as wide as a cart, and beyond it the river ran dark and fast.
    62	
    63	"It's shrunk," said Lira.
    64	
    65	"It's gone home. He said that it would, in the cold term." Brom had the mule's rope, and he was looking at the smith's chimney. "Half of them have fences of their own to sit behind."
    66	
    67	A cart was coming up the saddle road toward them, slowly, behind two mules, with a load under canvas that was white at the seams. Its driver walked at the near wheel. He was a round man, floured to the eyebrows, and he lifted a hand to them as carters did on a hill.
    68	
    69	Lira did not wait for him to draw level.
    70	
    71	"There's a healer walks a round out here. Which gate's she late at?"
