    76	
    77	"Millrace," said Karis. "I have it on the hide: a square with a dot, a day down the south road from this town."
    78	
    79	"A carter's day. Less, for legs." He looked at the brown mule with some attention, and then at the boxes, and Cael watched him decide that it was no business of his. "You'll want to be quick, if it's her you want. She's never more than two days at our fence."
    80	
    81	He clicked to his pair and went on up the hill.
    82	
    83	They stood with it on the hill for a breath. She had told them that if they wanted her sooner than Thornwater they were to ask a carter, and they had asked the first carter that they met, and she was a day from where they stood. Cael had expected to count gates for a week.
    84	
    85	"She said not without her," said Lira.
    86	
    87	"She did."
    88	
    89	"I'm only saying that it's a day south, and I don't know of anything we want to the south."
    90	
    91	"Nor do I," said Cael. "We'll hear what's on the oak."
    92	
    93	They went down into the meadow with the light going. The old place under the ash was empty, since a good many places were, and Brom unloaded there and rubbed the mule's back with a twist of hay while Seln walked once round the twelve fires and came back and said that there was nothing. A boy came for the standing-money. Karis gave him two coppers, which were too small a matter for her book.
    94	
    95	The plank under the lower inn's eave was bare of its book. Pike had taken it in for the night, as he did when the dusk came early; but a line of yellow showed under the inn's door, and the noise of the room came through the wood.
    96	
    97	"He's not said ours," said Brom. "The Fallow. He wrote it eleven days ago, and sent it up to us on a piece of bark, and it's not been said where we could hear it."
    98	
    99	"Then he'll say it," said Karis. "He has never yet kept one back."
   100	
   101	Cael put his hand to the latch.
   102	
   103	---
   104	
   105	Pike said it before the door was shut.
   106	
   107	He was on the hearth bench, under the sailcloth, with the ledger open beside him and the horn pot at his elbow; and his eyes came up as the cold came in. His look went over the five in the doorway once, and then a second time, as it went over everything. Then he turned his shoulder to the room and gave it out, in the voice that he used under his eave.
   108	
   109	It was the eighth line under their heading. Cael had read it on a scrap of bark in a grain hold, across the bones of a goat, and he knew every word; but he had never heard it said. Pike gave the recovery, and the Fallow road, and the broker's posting. He said that it was taken and witnessed. He said what had not been found there, and that the crew had come back. He gave the count of those who went in and of those who came out, which was the same count, and at the end of it he said that no price was paid.
   110	
   111	Then he said the whole of it again.
   112	
   113	Lira had not got the door shut. She stood with the latch in her right hand and the frost coming in round her boots, and nobody in that room told her to shut it.
   114	
   115	It was a thin house. There were perhaps twenty people on benches that had held forty, and every one of them had heard that line on the hundred and first day, when it was written. They had not then had the five to look at. Cael felt the room turn its attention on them as he had felt a yard do it once, at an academy on a hill. But this was no registry's look. Nobody's eyes went to where a tier ought to be.
   116	
   117	The hide-woman sat nearest the door, with a bundle of cured skins beside her on the bench end. She had given them a cake in their first week, and had told him over a wash that he was not paper. She said nothing now. She lifted the bundle off the bench and set it down between her feet, and went back to her cup.
   118	
   119	Three old men sat beyond her, at boards of their own. Cael had seen them under the ash for a hundred days without ever learning what they did, three grey heads over a pair of dice. The dice lay on the wood between them now. The eldest had his hand half closed over them, and he neither threw nor gathered them up.
   120	
   121	By the hearth sat the old carter who had come east with them from the first ford, and had asked Cael on a road whether he was paper. He had his chin lifted. He was looking past the five at the dark doorway behind them, steadily, as a man looks who wishes to see whether anything else is coming in.
   122	
