   193	*Priced too well. Seln says it's for the case. I say the floor's for me and they don't know it. South, tomorrow. Lira's arm is two weeks old. Brom says doors open both ways. He's quoting Rooke. He's never quoted anyone.*
   194	
   195	*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
   196	
   197	"*She was right*," said Lira. "You've put it in."
   198	
   199	"It's what happened."
   200	
   201	"I know that it is. I only wanted to see whether you'd write it when it was against you."
   202	
   203	---
   204	
   205	He carried the paper back up the bank alone.
   206	
   207	The room was thinner still: the three grey men had gone, and their dice with them, and a girl was turning the benches up along one wall. Pike sat where he had sat, under the cloth, with the fire fallen to red and the book shut on his knee.
   208	
   209	Cael laid the cream sheet on the bench beside him.
   210	
   211	"Taken."
   212	
   213	Pike looked at it once, and then at Cael, and then at the sheet again; and when he had done he opened the horn pot and dipped his pen without a word. He wrote five short strokes at the foot, side by side, as he had written them on another paper twenty-two days before.
   214	
   215	Then he wrote one thing more. He set it above the strokes, in the square capitals that he kept for it, and Cael read it upside down as it was made.
   216	
   217	*HOLD.*
   218	
   219	"I never asked for that," said Cael.
   220	
   221	"No." Pike blew on the ink. "Anybody can ask. I've said yes to one man in thirty years, and he's sitting behind you. This one I'm putting on myself."
   222	
   223	He got up, which he seldom did once he was down, and turned his shoulder to what was left of the room. He gave it out as he gave out a line: the recovery, and the Stair road, and the ridge, and the three hundred. He said that it was taken, and by whom. And then he said the other word, and he said it slowly.
   224	
   225	"Hold. On my own mark. If they're late down, nobody comes to me for that slip. It stays theirs till I've seen them or seen where they stopped." He sat down again. "That's said. I'll say it to the meadow in the morning."
   226	
   227	Cael stood with nothing in his hands, and he knew what that word was for: a captain asked for it so that a job should be kept for him. Pike had turned it round, as a healer had once said that her table was turned: he had put it on a paper so that if five people did not come back, nobody else should be sent up that ridge after three hundred marks to learn why.
   228	
   229	"Thank you," he said.
   230	
   231	"It's a line in a book. Don't thank a man for his book." Pike's one hand lay on the cover. "I said what I'd do with that paper. You've done the other. I'd as soon have you down to tell me which of us was right."
   232	
   233	Cael turned to go, and the man with the leather on his wrist was between him and the door.
   234	
   235	He had not heard him cross the room, though the man's three were on their feet behind him, at their distances, and it was the first time that Cael had stood within a stride of him since a river bank. He was no taller than Cael. He had grey over the ears and a hand-axe in his belt with its edge forward; and he kept his eyes on Cael's face and off his hands.
   236	
   237	"You're taking the Stair contract."
   238	
   239	"Yes," Cael said.
   240	
   241	"It's a bad contract."
   242	
   243	"Yes."
   244	
   245	The man nodded, once, by the width of a finger. He put no question as to why anybody would take a paper that he knew to be bad. Cael watched him weigh one and set it down again, as a thing that he had no means to price; and he saw the grey eyes go, for a breath, to the door, beyond which there was a meadow, and a fire, and a girl with one arm.
   246	
   247	"My crew's at Millrace in two days," Teague said. "If you're late back, we'll come look."
   248	
   249	He had said four things to Cael at a ford, and every one of them had been a price. This was not a price. Cael went over it for the catch, since he had been taught in a hard school to look for one, and there was none in it: there was a place, and a day, and four people who would walk up a ridge.
   250	
   251	He had said a certain word to registrars and to magisters, and to a hooded seat, whenever he was told a thing that he could neither fight nor thank them for. He had never once said it to anybody who stood above him on a wall. It was the only word that he had.
   252	
   253	"Noted."
   254	
   255	Teague stood aside from the door.
   256	
   257	---
   258	
   259	On that same night, the hundred and twelfth, Seln had the second watch, and he spent it reading two papers that were no longer in front of him.
   260	
   261	He needed no light for it, and he sat against the ash with his knees drawn up and the case on his chest, under his coat, where it had hung on every road past the Line; and the two sheets lay side by side in his head as plainly as they had lain on his knee. He could have counted the holes in either margin, and indeed he had done so, in the room, while a one-armed man was speaking.
   262	
   263	The frost was coming down, and he could hear it, very faintly, in the grass. Four people slept behind him, and a mule stood over three boxes; and at eleven other fires in that meadow there was nobody awake.
   264	
   265	He began with what he knew, which was the clerk.
   266	
   267	He had never met the man. He knew that he was no longer young, since his capitals were of a pattern that had not been taught for twenty years; that he ruled his own sheets and had been given a damaged wheel to do it with; and that he had been told what to write and nothing about why. Seln could hear the dictation in it. *South of Lowmarch; the first day.* A clerk left to himself would have put *one day south*. Somebody had stood at that man's shoulder with a surveyor's notes in his hand and read them out a stop at a time, and the clerk had set them down as they came.
   268	
   269	So there had been two people in that room, at the least: there was a hand that wrote, and a voice that knew the ground.
   270	
   271	He had spent fifteen years as the first of those. He knew what it was to write a true line at another person's bidding and not be told what the line was for. He had written sixty-three subjects into bundles in that way, each of them a person with a face that he could have drawn; and he had sent every bundle up a stair to people whom he had never once seen.
   272	
   273	That was the part of it that he had not been able to make four friends understand, and tonight he had not tried.
   274	
   275	He let himself go back to the corridor, which he allowed seldom. It was distempered green to the height of a man's shoulder, in a district seat three postings and twelve years behind him, and he had stopped dead in the middle of its floor with an armful of somebody's travel endorsements. A clerk had put his head out of a side door to ask whether he was unwell. He had not been unwell. He had understood, in the space between one step and the next, what a certain run of ledgers was that he had copied the year before as dull upkeep; and whose question it had been all along.
   276	
   277	After that there had been a press, in a records room on a top floor, and five weeks, and a case.
   278	
   279	Twelve years. He had kept it by never once describing it. He had carried it from posting to posting in the bottom of a satchel, under his shirts, and written *nothing to report* every week over the top of it; and in all that time he had never learned the name of one person who had made what was inside. He knew their paper. He knew how they folded it, and where they filed it, a little above where it should have sat, as only somebody could who was able to reach. He had their handwriting by heart, and it had not one face in it.
   280	
   281	He had been taught four grades of pressure, in their order. There was the record, and then the instrument, and then the knock, which was paper that nobody would own. Last came the hand, which was a road with no paper at all. He had believed for fifteen years that the hand was the worst of them. Then four people had come home to a hill from a chalk cut on the Ostrand road, and had told him what the hand looked like when it came: people with staves, who did their work without a word, and broke off in four heartbeats, and left nothing behind them but footmarks. He had told them that what stood in that road was a glove.
   282	
   283	A glove had something inside it. He had thought so then, and had kept it to himself, since it was not a finding.
   284	
   285	Now there was a fifth grade, which nobody had taught him, and he had held it in his two hands tonight. It was a lure. A lure was a different kind of thing from the other four. The record could be kept by nobody in particular, and the instrument read by any clerk; the knock came through a door and the hand out of a ditch. But something had to be done about a lure when it was taken. Somebody had to be there.
   286	
   287	*People have faces.*
   288	
   289	He had said it at a fire, to four people, in his own voice, and he was a little astonished at himself still. He went over it now as he would have gone over a return before it was sealed, to see whether it was true or only well put.
   290	
   291	---
   292	
   293	It was not revenge, though he looked for that first, since it was the commonest thing. He turned out every pocket of it, and found that he did not want anybody on that rim hurt, and had no wish to see a hood pulled back by force. He had spent too long being the quiet man at the edge of a room to suppose that the people who would wait at the foot of that ridge were anything but quiet men at the edge of somebody else's room, sent by a stair that they had never climbed.
   294	
   295	What he wanted was smaller than that, and he thought that it was worse.
   296	
   297	He wanted to know that it was done by persons.
   298	
   299	For twelve years the thing in the case had been like weather to him. It had fallen on forty children, and on a good many more before them, as rain falls on a field; and a man does not go looking for the face of rain. He files what it did; and Seln had filed it, and had been very good at that, and it had let him sleep. But a boy had come up a hill with a word on his file where a tier should have been, and had looked at every instrument that was turned on him as though somebody had built it. That had been the end of the weather. If a thing was built, then there was a builder, with a walk, and a way of holding a pen, and a tiredness at the end of the day. Seln found that he could no longer do without seeing one. He wished to stand on the same ground as a single one of them, in daylight, and learn what a person looked like who did this for his living.
   300	
   301	It was a clerk's want, since a file wanted a name at its head; he did not think that it was a noble one, and he entered it as it stood.
   302	
   303	Then he audited his vote, since he owed that.
   304	
   305	He had been asked what he would do, and he had answered for himself, and he had said that it should count for one hand. All of that was correct. It was also true that four people had heard him want something for the first time in two years, and that three of them were of an age to be moved by it. He had seen the researcher shut her mouth on a question. He had seen the big one look at him as he looked at a load that had shifted. Had he put his thumb on the scale? He thought not. The boy had ruled on his own ground, and the boy's reason was better than his: it was about what the paper did not know, and it would have stood if Seln had never spoken.
   306	
   307	He was not certain. He wrote that down too, in the place where he kept such things, which was nowhere.
   308	
   309	There was a way to be certain, and he had been looking at it since the fire was banked.
   310	
   311	The paper followed the case. He had said so himself. If the case were to go south tonight, by itself, on one man's chest, then by first light there would be nothing at this fire that anybody in the west had priced. Five people would wake to a walk at fifty and a fence at forty. He knew the south road; he had walked the first hours of it alone in their second week here, as he had walked every road out of this town. He could be on the ridge before the stars turned, and he would have his face, very likely; and an arm two weeks old would never come within a mile of that floor.
   312	
   313	His hand was on the strap, and he let it stay there, and looked at the thing squarely.
   314	
   315	He had lost an argument on a shelf above a valley with no name, on the second night past the Line. He had proposed that the watches should be his to arrange as he judged best, since he had fifteen years of judging them; and a girl with a ruled table had told him that the rota was not set aside for anybody's judgment, his own included. He had been angry for an hour and grateful ever since. A man who has decided things alone for fifteen years does not stop because he has resolved to; he stops when four people will not let him.
   316	
   317	And there was the other reason, which he had given a boy in a loft. He had told him that he would sooner see a healer warned of what might be done to her than be used as the reason for keeping her ignorant. If that was good for a healer, it was good for a crew. He could not carry the danger off in the night and leave them to find a cold place under a tree. That was the service's way. It had been his own way, every week, for fifteen years: to know what was best for a subject, and arrange it, and say nothing.
   318	
   319	He had resigned from that. He had merely never filed it.
   320	
   321	Four hands had gone up at that fire, and one had stayed down, and had asked to be entered, and was going regardless. He was not entitled to take a decision out of five people's hands by removing its object, however kindly he meant it.
   322	
   323	He took his own hand off the strap.
   324	
   325	The case lay where it had lain, against his breastbone, with its lock toward him. He knew what was in it to the leaf. He had not opened it since a reading room above Ostrand, and he did not open it now; there was nothing in it that would tell him who was waiting at the foot of a ridge, and there was one leaf in it that was not a copy.
   326	
   327	The stars had turned as far as they turned in one watch, and he got up, without a sound, and went round the four sleepers, and the boxes, and the mule, as he had gone round every camp of theirs since the shelf. Then he bent and touched the girl's right shoulder with two fingers. He chose that shoulder with care.
   328	
   329	She woke at once, as she did everything.
   330	
   331	"Anything?" said Lira.
   332	
   333	"Nothing," said Seln.
   334	
   335	It was the report that he gave at the end of every watch. She took the blanket that he held out, and put her back against the ash, and he lay down where she had been lying, which was still warm.

