     1	# Chapter 47 — Millrace
     2	
     3	They were out of Lowmarch two hours before the light on the hundred and thirteenth day, with the stars still hard over the river and every blade in the meadow standing white.
     4	
     5	It was Seln's hour, and Cael had given it to him without argument. A loaded cart took the whole of a short day to reach the mill; a crew that walked as they walked could be on the ridge by sunrise and at the fork before noon, and Seln wished to have the afternoon in hand. So Brom loaded the mule by feel. Lira ate standing. They went past Pike's bare plank and the smith's cold chimney, and took the south road in file, with the frost ringing under five pairs of boots and four small hooves.
     6	
     7	They passed the well at the fork in the dark. It had a new rope on its windlass, and somebody had mended the curb where six stones had been set; and Cael, who had hauled foul air out of that shaft for three days on a bitten leg, put his hand on the stonework as he went by.
     8	
     9	The road climbed from there. By the time there was grey enough to see by they were on the back of a long ridge, bare and broad, with pale grass on it that the wind had combed all one way. The country fell off on both hands. To the east it was hills beyond hills, hatched with the red-barked trees that Karis would not name. To the west the ground went down in long folds toward a valley that he could not yet see into, and a line of mist lay along the bottom of it like wool caught on a fence.
    10	
    11	"That's water," said Lira. "Under the white. It's the same that she walked down."
    12	
    13	"The mill water," said Karis. She had the calfskin half unrolled against her chest, and she was reading it as she walked. "He draws it with three strokes where it is forded and with one where it is not. It comes round out of the hills to the north and east. A person leaving that hold by its northern wicket would meet it on the second morning, and it would bring her here."
    14	
    15	The sun came up on their left hand, small and white. It showed them their own breath, and the mule's, and a road that ran on south along the ridge as far as they could see; and a little before the middle of the forenoon it showed them a place where the ruts divided.
    16	
    17	One pair kept the ridge. The other turned down the western face, slantwise, through a notch that wheels had worn, and went out of sight.
    18	
    19	"The fork," said Seln. He had walked the first hours of that road by himself, in their second week at Lowmarch, and had never said what he found. "The left hand is ours, later. The right is the mill."
    20	
    21	They heard the wheel before they saw a roof.
    22	
    23	It came up to them out of the notch while they were still among the rocks: a slow heavy knocking, with water falling under it, at about the pace of a tired man splitting wood. Brom stopped the mule to listen. Then the track came round a shoulder, and the valley opened.
    24	
    25	It was a narrow green place, a good deal greener than anything on the ridge. The water came down it fast and brown over a weir of stakes and stone, and above the weir a channel had been cut away along the bank to a tall grey building with a wheel on its side as high as a barn door. The wheel went round steadily in a white fall of water. There was a fence of split posts about the mill and a dozen roofs, and below the fence a ford, with a flat stone on each bank for the wheels to aim at. Smoke rose straight up from four chimneys.
    26	
    27	A woman was sitting on the nearer of the flat stones with a net of some kind across her knees. She raised her head at the sound of hooves, and counted them down the track with her chin, one at a time, as Pike counted a meadow. Then she turned and called toward the mill, without getting up, in a voice that had been pitched to carry over a wheel.
    28	
    29	"Five off the ridge."
    30	
    31	The answer came from a lean-to that had been built against the mill's southern wall, where the sun fell; and it came at once, low and hoarse, from somebody who had not troubled to come to the door.
    32	
    33	"I know."
    34	
    35	---
    36	
    37	There was a bench along the mill's wall, and they sat down on the end of it.
    38	
    39	She had told him at a well that if she was at a table when he found her, he would sit and wait his turn like anybody; and he had not understood, until he had the planks under him, what a plain relief it would be to do a thing exactly as it had been set. Nobody on that bench looked at him twice. There were fifteen people on it, or had been at first light; nine were left, and two more stood about the door with their caps in their hands.
    40	
    41	The lean-to had no front. It was three walls of board and a roof of turf, with the mill's warm stone for its back; she had her plank on two kegs in the middle of it, and her things laid out along a shelf as they had lain on a sill at Thornwater. He could see the whole of her work from where he sat, and he watched it as he had once watched a sixth-year's bout from a rail.
    42	
    43	She had a man on the plank when they came, with his right hand in her two.
    44	
    45	It was a bad hand. He was one of the miller's men, and a rope had taken it nine days before, against the drum of the hoist that lifted the sacks; it had been bound up in a meal-bag since, and it lay in her palms like a thing that had been trodden on. She had her eyes shut. Her lips were moving, and Cael found that he knew where she was by the count: at three she had the banks, and by seven she had gone along the bed to where it was stopped. He had lain under those hands for twenty minutes. He had a fragment in him of what she was doing, the tenth, and it sat quiet under his breastbone and did not stir toward her, since it stopped at the length of his own arm.
    46	
    47	She had been at it for most of an hour before they came, by the grey of the man's face, and she held it a quarter of an hour longer. When she laid it down on the man's chest, it had five fingers that he could move, and he was weeping without any noise.
    48	
    49	"That's one," said Oryn. "You'll not close it on a rope before the thaw. Send in the child."
    50	
    51	It was a girl of three or four, carried in by her father, with a wet rattling breath that Cael could hear from the bench. The healer did not put her on the plank. She sat down on a keg and took the child on her own knee, with one broad hand spread on the small back and the other on the breast, and bent her cropped head until her ear was against the shoulder-blades. She stayed so for a long time.
    52	
    53	He saw her face over the child's hair. There was another chest, at another gate, that she had last listened to on the eighty-ninth evening. She had counted the days to it on a hill, aloud, with nobody by. None of that showed. She had one child under her hands, and she gave it the whole of herself, as she gave everything.
    54	
    55	"It's low down, and on the one side," she said to the father at last. "It's not what you were afraid of. Hold her so. She'll cough, and you're to let her."
    56	
    57	The mending of that took a good deal less time than the hand, and at the end of it the child coughed until she was red, and then slept.
    58	
    59	"That's two."
    60	
    61	The third came in on a hurdle.
    62	
    63	She was an old woman, very small and very upright, with two grandsons to carry her and a stick laid along her side that she plainly meant to walk home on. Her left knee was the size of a loaf under her skirt. The healer looked at it from her stride and a half, and then at its owner, and something came into her tired face that Cael had seen there only once before, at a board with a goat on it.
    64	
    65	"You were to wait for me on your own doorstep. I'd put a day by for it."
    66	
    67	"I waited sixteen of them," said the old woman. "Then I heard you were at the mill. I'd sooner be carried down a valley than sit another night with it."
    68	
    69	"What did you do?"
    70	
    71	"Nothing. I was getting a ham down."
    72	
    73	"Off a ladder." Oryn had her hands round the joint already. "It's got a ladder in it. I can feel every rung. You went through the fourth, and you came down on this with the rest of you after."
    74	
    75	"The third."
    76	
    77	"It was the fourth. Lie back."
    78	
    79	That one took her until the shadow of the wheel lay across the door, and when it was done she sat on her keg with her head down between her shoulders for as long as it takes to count fifty.
    80	
    81	"That's three," she said to the floor. "That's the day."
    82	
    83	She had told them on a mountain that her reserve was a purse, and that three such mendings emptied it.
    84	
    85	She did the rest of them with one hand each, and that took her less time than the knee had. Cael counted a dozen. There was a carter with a wrenched back, and a boy with a stye, and a woman whose tooth she looked at and would not touch. She laid two fingers on a wrist, or a palm on a forehead, for a count of ten, and told each of them the truth in a sentence. She put tape on a thumb. She told a big sullen man that there was nothing wrong with him that a week away from his brother would not cure, and the whole bench laughed, the man included.
    86	
    87	Then there was nobody between the five and the door.
    88	
    89	---
    90	
    91	"You," said Oryn.
    92	
    93	She was speaking to him. She had washed to the elbow in a bucket, and she stood drying her hands on her breeches, a stride and a half inside the lean-to, with the old woman's hurdle going away up the valley behind her.
    94	
    95	He got up off the bench and went to the edge of the turf roof's shadow, and stopped there.
    96	
    97	"How many?"
    98	
    99	"One. The same one. I've not gone back to it."
   100	
   101	"Then you've nothing for me yet, and you know it, and you sat an hour on my bench all the same." Her eyes went over him as they had gone over him in a yard. "So it's something else. Say it."
   102	
   103	He had turned it over on the ridge since before the sun was up, and it had seemed a plain enough thing there. It was harder in front of her, with her sleeves pushed back and three mendings gone out of her since first light.
   104	
   105	"I've read one thing with what I had from you, and it was myself. I found no bank in it anywhere, as you found none. I don't know what a bank is like under the hand. I've only your word that there is such a thing." He kept his own hands at his sides. "I'd go back to it better if I had felt one. So I'm asking whether I may read you. Here, or afterward, or on a day you name. The short one, and no more than you choose."
   106	
   107	He heard Lira's breath stop, on the bench behind him.
   108	
   109	The healer did not answer at once. He saw her look at his right hand, where it hung, and then at her own wrist; and for the space of one breath there was the hunger in her face that he had seen at a ford and in a yard, when she had wanted to know a thing more than she wanted her supper. She put it by as he had seen her put it by before.
   110	
   111	"No."
   112	
   113	She said it without heat. She hung the cloth on its nail, and turned back to him.
   114	
   115	"You asked. That's the rule and you kept it. The answer's no. Next."
   116	
   117	He stood for as long as it took him to be sure that he had nothing to add, and he had nothing. He had asked a person for the first time in his life whether he might do a thing to her; she had said that he might not; and the whole of what was left to him was to go and sit down. He went and sat down. It was not hard to do, and he was a little surprised at that, since he had dreaded it.
   118	
   119	Lira was getting to her feet beside him, with her right hand already at the knot of her sling.
   120	
   121	He took Hesk's book out of the satchel. He had written nothing in it yet that day, and he wetted the pencil and put this down before anything else, with the date over it, so that it should stand first.
   122	
   123	*Hundred and thirteenth day. Millrace, the healer's bench, a little after noon. Asked her whether I might read her. It is the first time that I have asked anybody. She said no. Entered before anything else today, since it is the first of its kind and I want it to head the leaf.*
   124	
   125	Karis had been reading it upside down, from his other side.
   126	
   127	"She looked at your hand before she said it," Karis murmured. Her voice was pitched for his ear alone. "And then at her own wrist. I have seen that look on a person in front of a shut press. She was curious about you. Enter that somewhere."
   128	
   129	"It's not mine to enter."
   130	
   131	"It is an observation. You may put my initial against it."
   132	
   133	He put it down, with her initial; and he had hardly lifted the pencil when a hoarse voice from the lean-to said that the arm had taken no harm on two days of frozen road, that all eight were as they had been, and that the knot had been retied by somebody who tied like a carter.
   134	
   135	"That was me," said Brom, from the bench. "At the ford. She'd got it wet."
   136	
   137	"I can see who it was. Hand."
   138	
   139	He gave her his hand across the sill, and she turned it over and gave it back. She put two fingers on Karis's throat. She gave Seln her short nod. In the time that it takes to draw a bucket she had gone round the five of them and found nothing that wanted her, and she sat down on her keg with her forearms on her knees.
   140	
   141	"That's my bench, then. Which of you is going to tell me why you're a day south of your board, with a mule of mine and three boxes, on a road that goes nowhere but a ford?"
   142	
   143	---
   144	
