   239	The sound of his own breath stopped. That was how it came: the world went on exactly as before, and he went out of it. He lifted his boot to the third step and set it down, and no stone answered, and he could not hear his coat. Far below him he heard a small sharp breath that was Lira's, since she had been looking at a boy on a stair, and now she was looking at a stair.
   240	
   241	He went up over the lip and stood on the floor.
   242	
   243	He had made up his mind about the next thing in the dark. There were ten bolts lying on that grey somewhere, where they had skated and stopped, and one kick would send any of them ringing; so he gave the read the ground, which was the oldest and quietest thing that he owned, and it went out under his soles for its pace and a half, as it always did. It told him what was there, and did not tell him what anything meant. But a spent bolt has a weight, and he felt the first of them lying a yard ahead and a little to the left, like a pencil on a table, before his boot could find it.
   244	
   245	He stepped over it.
   246	
   247	It took the whole of his attention, and he saw that at once, and saw the price of it. He was a boy walking across thirty yards of bare stone in the last of the light, with six people looking at that stone; and he could not raise his eyes to them. He had them in his head only: two on the brow in front of him, two away on his left, one on his right, and one behind, where the west was still green. He walked with his eyes in the ground, and stepped over four bolts, and went round a fifth.
   248	
   249	Then there was frozen grass under him, and the old road.
   250	
   251	He took the southern of its two ruts, since that was the side that the newer bar stood on. It was a trough a foot deep and as wide as a wheel, with turf grown down into it, and it ran straight up the fall of the ground to a stone that he could not yet see; and he had a hundred and ninety yards of it to walk.
   252	
   253	The cold began at fifty.
   254	
   255	He had been waiting for it. A thing made for hiding costs from the moment that a person wakes; he had paid that on every day since it came to him, by carrying it shut. To have it open was a different bill. It began in the ends of his fingers, as it had begun in a lane on the Fallow, and it was not the frost, since it came from inside. At fifty strides he could not feel his nails; at a hundred his hands hung at the ends of his arms like things that he was carrying for somebody, and the cold had gone into his wrists.
   256	
   257	He looked up once, at a hundred and twenty.
   258	
   259	He could not have said why, and he paid for it at once. The brow stood against the eastern sky, dark, and on it there were two shapes. One was sitting, with something long laid across its knees; the other stood a little to the south of the first, with its hands drawn up inside its sleeves, as a person stands who has need of his fingers and means to keep them warm.
   260	
   261	The read went out from under his feet. He had taken his mind off it for the length of one glance, and his boot came down on a loose stone in the rut and turned it over.
   262	
   263	It made no sound, since nothing that he did made any sound. But he stood for ten breaths with his heart going, and his eyes in the ground again, and gave the read back to the turf before he took another step.
   264	
   265	At a hundred and fifty there was a noise that nearly stopped that heart altogether.
   266	
   267	It was the grey mule. She was hobbled above the stone with the brown one, and she had put up her head and brayed at the dusk, long and rusty, as she had no doubt brayed at every dusk of her life. The brown mule did not bray. She had turned her ears, both together, down the old road, and she was looking straight at the place where he stood.
   268	
   269	On the brow, the sitting shape turned its hood toward the two beasts.
   270	
   271	It looked at them for as long as it takes to count five, and saw a mule that was making a noise and a mule that was not, and three boxes under a blanket. Then the hood came round again to the floor, and to the square hole in the middle of it, where nothing had stirred all day.
   272	
   273	Cael walked on, and he could not feel his arms below the elbow.
   274	
   275	---
   276	
   277	At fourteen feet from the newer bar there was nothing.
   278	
   279	He had known that there would not be. He could see the iron itself, a dark stroke above the frosted turf, and the grey stone beyond it; and the air between that bar and its fellow was as empty as any air. He took a stride, and another, and let Anchor look.
   280	
   281	At twelve feet there was the greyness, like breath on glass. At ten, they were there.
   282	
   283	There were three courses across the old road, at the knee and the chest and over the head, exactly as he had seen them thrown in the morning; they were as thick as his wrist, and they had not slackened in a day. He went along them with that other sight to the newer bar, where he had watched them arrive, and looked for the place where they were made fast.
   284	
   285	It was not there.
   286	
   287	He stood in a ditch with the cold in his elbows and looked again. The three courses came to the newer bar and touched it, and there was a point where each of them met the iron; but they did not end. They went through those points as a rope goes through a ring, and on, southward along the edge, out of his sight.
   288	
   289	He had got it wrong. He had watched three lines fly across a road toward a bar, and had taken the bar for the end of them. A rope is not made fast where it lands; it is made fast where the hauling starts.
   290	
   291	He turned his head, slowly, to the north.
   292	
   293	The old bar stood eight feet away across the grown-over road, under its five cold terms of rust. He was eleven feet from it, perhaps, where he stood, and at that distance he could make out only that there was something on it. He climbed out of the southern rut, and crossed the crown of the old road on his hands and knees, since the turf there stood a foot higher and he did not trust the thing that hid him to hide him against the sky; and he let himself down into the northern rut.
   294	
   295	It was there.
   296	
   297	It was on the far side of the iron, the side toward the brow, a hand above the turf, and he saw it through the bar, as a person sees a lamp through his own fingers. It was a single point, as big as a fist. Three courses came into it from the south, across the road; and three came into it from the north, round the whole of that great circle, the other end of the same rope. Nothing came out. Everything that held six people in a low place on the hundred and fourteenth day was hauled taut through that one point and belayed there, like the whole of a ship's rigging brought down to a single pin.
   298	
   299	He could see it. He had been able to see a line across a doorway once, with a girl's shoulder pressed against it, and it had done him no good whatever.
   300	
   301	He lay down in the rut on his left side, with the bar at the length of his arm. He put his right hand out toward it, and round it, and could not tell when he had touched it; he had to watch his own fingers close on the scale to learn that they had. Past the iron, his hand was on the far side of that edge, and he laid the dead palm against the outer face, low down.
   302	
   303	It was against him now, as the first had been against his coat; and he was inside what it held.
   304	
   305	*A thing set against a surface and told to stay.*
   306	
   307	He told it not to.
   308	
   309	It went as a breath goes that a person has been holding, and the point was simply not under his palm. The three courses to the south dropped across the old road, and he saw the nearest ten feet of them go, like wet rope falling; and from the north, away round the dark edge of the low ground, he felt the rest come in and go slack and run out through the place where a knot had been, a great deal of it, for as long as it takes to draw a bucket. He had not known how much ground it held until it stopped holding.
   310	
   311	Forty paces above him, on the brow, a standing shape flung both its arms forward out of its sleeves, as a man does when a rope is dragged through his hands.

