     1	# Chapter 51 — Declaring
     2	
     3	He let Shadow go while he lay in the ditch, with his arm still round a bar of the registry's iron.
     4	
     5	It went out of him as it had come. The sound of his own breath returned, loud and ragged, and after it the creak of frozen grass under his hip and the grey mule shifting her feet above the stone. He was a boy lying in an old rut at dusk, under the turf's lip, where nobody on that brow could have seen more of him than a shoulder; and whatever they had looked at for the last quarter of an hour, it had not been him.
     6	
     7	He drew his arm back. He could not feel it. Both his hands were dead to the elbow, with a cold that had nothing to do with the frost, and when he pushed himself up on them it was like leaning on two posts.
     8	
     9	He stood.
    10	
    11	The six on the rim were where they had been all day: two on the brow above him, two away to the south, one north, and one far off across the low ground against the last of the light. The two on the brow were on their feet; the one with the bow had it up across his chest, spanned, and the other was holding his own hands out before him and looking into them.
    12	
    13	Cael stepped out of the rut and walked across the edge, between the old bar and the stone.
    14	
    15	He did it slowly, since he wished it seen. He set his boots down on the outside of a line that five people could not cross that morning, and turned his face up the old road to the brow, and stood there with his arms hanging. It was forty paces. In that light he was a dark upright thing against paler grass, and the man above him had hit a shoulder at five paces more.
    16	
    17	The bow came round.
    18	
    19	He had priced this on the stair. There was one bolt in that groove, and when it was gone, a boot must go into a stirrup and a hook onto a string, and a back must straighten; he had watched that man do it once, from a frozen road, and it had taken him something over twenty counts. One bolt, then, and after it twenty counts in which a bow was a stick. He had been told on a bluff never to spend the Wind near a thing whose single blow he could not price. He could price this one to a hair.
    20	
    21	He set his feet as Lira had taught him to set them.
    22	
    23	*Front. Back. Shoulders.* He loaded nothing on the third. He stood through it, with Wind lying quiet in his soles, and he watched a hooded shape forty paces up a hill. A girl had come down off a gate-walk eight days ago with a sore palm and three words, which a lean man had given her. Shooting was stopping. The stock on the brow settled into a shoulder. The shape behind it grew smaller, as a person does who is letting out his breath. And then it was entirely still.
    24	
    25	*Four, and.*
    26	
    27	The air cracked like a whip over the stone, and the frost went up off the grass in a white fan. He was twenty feet to the north, in pale stems to the knee, and something had gone through the place where he had been with a sound like cloth tearing. He heard it strike the frozen road, a long way down behind him.
    28	
    29	The landing locked.
    30	
    31	*One.* Everything that he owned went dumb. For the length of that count there was a boy standing on a hillside who could not have shifted a foot to save his life; and he spent it looking up at the brow, since his eyes were the only part of him that would answer. The man with the bow had not stood to watch his bolt. He had the nose of the thing on the turf already, and his boot going into the stirrup.
    32	
    33	Then the count was done, and Cael ran.
    34	
    35	He went up the slope on a slant, toward the old road, with the frozen grass breaking under him and forty paces of hill before him, and his hands swinging at the ends of his arms like things that he had borrowed. He counted as he ran, and it was the bowman's count that he kept and not his own: at seven the hook was on the string, and at eleven the back was beginning to come up.
    36	
    37	At twelve yards short of the brow he reached the lip of the old rut, and he gave up the ground.
    38	
    39	It was like shutting an eye. The pace and a half that had been under his soles since a stair went out from under them. He let Storm, the heaviest thing that he owned, have the air instead, and he laid the lane up the rut.
    40	
    41	It was a corridor as long as a cart and team, set down in that trough with its high end at his feet and its low end up the hill, and everything in it went one way at once. Frost went, and the seed that hung heavy on the grass at either lip, and chaff, and the dust of a hundred dry days; it went up that channel in a grey flood and burst out over the brow, twelve yards on, into two hoods.
    42	
    43	He let it go, and began to pay.
    44	
    45	---
    46	
    47	For ten counts after a lane, everything that he had came to him a beat late.
    48	
    49	He had run up a ditch on the Fallow in that lag, with hounds behind him, and he knew it; but he had not known what it was to close with a man in it. His boots struck the turf, and then he was told that they had struck. The brow came up to meet him. There was a hooded shape in a cloud of chaff with one arm across its face, and it had let the string go half drawn and caught the bow up by its stirrup end.
    50	
    51	He saw the stock begin to move. Then he saw where it was, which was a good deal nearer.
    52	
    53	It was coming round at his head like a flail. He got his right arm up. It was a dead arm, and it was late, and for the first beat nothing in him answered at all; a pound of ash wood and horn met the bone of his forearm with the whole of a strong man's swing behind it. His knees began to go.
    54	
    55	Then Compression, which a fighter had put through him on a circuit, long ago, arrived and did what it was for.
    56	
    57	There was only one road open to it. The blow went down the bone into his shoulder, and down his spine, and through both his heels into the hill; and the hill took it. The stock stopped against his arm as it would have stopped against a gatepost. He felt it ring from the wrist to the elbow, and he knew that he would be paying for that for a week.
    58	
    59	The hooded man had both his hands stung off the bow. It fell between them.
    60	
    61	Cael did not strike him. He could have; the man stood open, a yard off, with his cloth blown half across his hood and grass-seed in the folds of it, and there was nothing in that hood that Cael could have sworn to afterward but that it was the height of a person. He looked at it for the length of one late breath, and turned away from it, to the south.
    62	
    63	The other stood two yards off.
    64	
    65	He had not run, and he had not reached for anything, since he carried nothing to reach for. He stood with his feet apart on the trodden ruts, and both hands held out before his chest, bare, with the palms turned toward Cael and the fingers a little spread. They were quite still.
    66	
    67	Cael knew that stillness. He had knelt in a chalk cut with nineteen points round him, and had watched a woman make it fifteen feet away, with the whole of her attention gone down into the ends of her fingers; it was the stillness of threading a needle. In another breath there would be something set against the front of his coat, and told to stay.
    68	
    69	He did not let Anchor look. He had no need to; he had the hands.
    70	
    71	They were a man's hands, broad, and short in the finger. That was all that he was ever to know of their owner. There was a pale band round the third finger of the left, where a ring had been taken off before the work began.
    72	
    73	His own left hand had found his knife. He could not feel the hilt. He drew it, and held it out at the full length of his arm, with the point a hand's breadth under those two open palms; and he asked Ember for the one thing that it had.
    74	
    75	A point of fire stood on the knife's tip.
    76	
    77	It was no bigger than a candle's. It lit the palms from beneath, and the grey cuffs, and nothing above them. He held it there. He said no word, and he did not move the blade nearer by the width of a hair.
    78	
    79	A person cannot thread a needle with a flame under his hands.
    80	
    81	He saw the fingers want to. He saw them hold their stillness for one breath, and for a second, with the heat coming up into them. Then they closed, slowly, and the hands drew back, and went down to the man's sides.
    82	
    83	The man took one step backward. He took another. He did not turn his hood away from the flame until he was five yards off; and Cael did not follow him by a single stride.
    84	
    85	It was the bowman who ended it. He had got the chaff out of his cloth, and he bent, without haste, and took up the bow from the turf. He did something to the lock of it with his thumb, and a small dark thing like a walnut came away into his palm and went into his coat. He drew a short knife across the string, and then he laid the bow down again in the ruts, carefully, on its side, as a workman lays down a tool that was only lent him.
    86	
    87	The two of them went north along the carters' road, side by side, at a trot. They did not run until they were sixty yards off.
    88	
    89	Cael turned to the low ground, and lifted the knife over his head.
    90	
    91	The lag had gone out of him. He stood on the brow in the last of the dusk, with a point of fire at the end of his arm where anybody on that rim could see it, and he looked for the other four.
    92	
    93	To the south there were two shapes still. One of them, the one that had stood all day with its arms folded, raised a single arm above its hood and let it fall. Then both stooped, and straightened, and went away over the rim to the south, with nothing in their hands.
    94	
    95	When he looked to the north, there was nobody there at all.
    96	
    97	The west was the last. The sky there was green yet, low down, and the rim stood against it as black as a cut edge. A small upright shape had stood on that edge since first light and had never once loosed. It bent now, and laid something down. It walked away; and at the top of the rim, where another step would have taken it out of his sight, it stopped, and turned itself round, and looked back across five hundred yards of grass at a boy with a light.
    98	
    99	He saw the hood against the sky. There was the shape of it, and the shoulders under it, and inside it there was nothing whatever. Then that was gone too.
   100	
   101	Six. He counted them off that rim as Seln had counted them onto it. He let the fire go out, and walked down forty paces to the grey stone, and called one word down the old road into the dark.
   102	
   103	"Back."
   104	
   105	---
