   108	
   109	He heard them long before he saw them. There was no need for quiet now. Brom's breathing came first, heavy and careful, and then a shuffle of boots on frozen turf; and at last five shapes grew out of the dusk on the old road, with a small one in front that made no sound at all.
   110	
   111	Seln stepped over the stone, and stood a breath on the outside of it. Cael saw his shoulders come down by the width of a finger, as a man's do when a Path that he has done without all day is handed back to him.
   112	
   113	Karis was next, and she had said that she would never grow used to it. She crossed, and stopped, and put out her right hand in front of her in the dark. A point of light stood on the air above her palm, as small and steady as a star.
   114	
   115	"I have a hand," she said, to nobody. Her voice shook. "I have a hand again."
   116	
   117	She set the point on the wick of her lamp.
   118	
   119	The healer came over between two of them, with Brom's good arm across her shoulders and his weight on her; and Cael watched her do what he had seen her do at a rock on the Fallow, eight times in one grey morning, when she thought that nobody saw. She took one stride past the stone. She stopped dead, with her eyes shut and her lips moving, and her free hand closed on Brom's wrist.
   120	
   121	The count went to four, and on, and did not stop.
   122	
