   156	
   157	He paid the rest of his own bill sitting against the old iron, with his knees drawn up.
   158	
   159	It came all at once, as it had come at the stone on the Fallow. His right forearm was dead from the wrist to the elbow, where a stock had been stopped, and across his left palm there was a white weal as long as a finger. He had held a knife with a flame on its point in a hand too cold to know that the hilt was hot, and he had found it out only when he tried to open that hand again. He could not stop shaking. He had had a week of barley sacks and tables to rest in, and he had spent the whole of what it gave him between a stair and a brow, in less time than a kettle takes.
   160	
   161	The healer looked at the palm from her stride and a half.
   162	
   163	"I can't touch that. You know why." She threw him what was left of Lira's tape. "Wind it round, and keep it out of the frost. It'll close faster than it ought to. Yours always do."
   164	
   165	Seln had gone to the mules.
   166	
   167	He went round the three boxes under their blanket with the lamp held low, and stooped over each cord in turn, and touched nothing. When he came back his face was as it had been all day.
   168	
   169	"I tied those to be read. There is a stem of grass laid under every knot, and each is where I laid it." He set the lamp down. "Nobody has had a hand on them. I had thought that it would be so. Six people with a quarter of a mile of rim apiece do not leave it to look into a mule's load."
   170	
   171	"What did they see?" said Cael. "Tell me as you'd write it for them."
   172	
   173	"I have been composing it since you called." Seln folded his hands. "A fence was laid round that ground before light, and shut behind six people after sunrise. It held all day. At dusk it failed, at its knot, which was set on an iron bar that stands upon the very edge of the place. Directly afterward a boy stood up out of the old road beside that bar and walked across the edge. He declared a Wind, and something heavy in a ditch, and a fire, and he took a blow that ought to have broken his arm."
   174	
   175	"And between the stair and the bar?"
   176	
