   178	
   179	He turned, and went up the old road to the brow by himself.
   180	
   181	When he came down he had a crossbow in his hands.
   182	
   183	He brought it into the lamplight and turned it over, slowly, as he turned over any paper. It was a good bow, heavier than the healer's, with a prod of horn and sinew. The string hung from one end of it, cut, and the lock was an empty socket. He ran his thumb along the underside of the stock, and under the stirrup, and across the butt.
   184	
   185	"There was a maker's stamp here. It has been taken off with a file, and the place oiled. There is no armoury number. It was bought for one day, by somebody who meant from the first to leave it lying." His voice had not risen. Cael had never heard it so quiet. "I walked past the place where that man lay, twice. I have sat in a hole since sunrise with his bolt on the stair. I have his bow. And I could not tell you tonight whether he was tall."
   186	
   187	He took the thing by its two ends.
   188	
   189	Cael had known him for two years. He had seen him carve a goat, and wax a seal, and stand in a doorway with his back to a room, and he had never seen him do a thing in anger. Seln lifted the crossbow over his head and brought it down across the grey stone with the whole of his narrow strength, and the stock broke in two at the lock. He stood over it, breathing.
   190	
   191	"No faces."
