+
+Scene numbers follow the manuscript's `---` divisions and the sweep probe. Line ranges are: chapter 45, 1–54 / 56–102 / 104–140 / 142–174 / 176–227; chapter 46, 1–48 / 50–104 / 106–148 / 150–202 / 204–256 / 258–290 / 292–335; chapter 47, 1–34 / 36–88 / 90–142 / 144–194 / 196–247; chapter 48, 1–52 / 54–122 / 124–170 / 172–234 / 236–285; chapter 49, 1–40 / 42–88 / 90–140 / 142–188 / 190–242 / 244–315; chapter 50, 1–60 / 62–106 / 108–154 / 156–226 / 228–274 / 276–311; chapter 51, 1–44 / 46–104 / 106–154 / 156–226 / 228–260 / 262–328 / 330–383; chapter 52, 1–60 / 62–104 / 106–164 / 166–230 / 232–300 / 302–369.
+
+## Tracked passages
+
+| chapter:line | Manuscript opening words | Source sentence followed | Kind | Beat chain to break without opening the source |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 52:69 | “At the twentieth the air changed…” | `books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:23` — “The air changed at the twentieth step…” | near-verbatim | The packet requires an air change at the twentieth step and two lamps. Beyond that, both sentences make the change arrive at that numbered step, make both flames stand straight and still, move to the colder air, and finish by inventorying the missing smells of earth, water, roots or growing things. Break the chain by letting the changed flame be noticed before the step is counted, or carry the cold and absent smells into the chamber and let another character identify them later rather than listing them on the stair. |
+| 52:77 | “Cael catalogued the room properly…” | `books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:27` — “He catalogued the chamber by lamp, properly…” | near-verbatim | Both sentences announce a proper catalogue and immediately justify it by contrasting this attempt with the rushed or impossible look on the previous day. The plan requires a proper catalogue, but not this same **proper catalogue → yesterday's failed catalogue** sentence frame. Enter on Cael's first measurement or on the dust taking the lantern light; disclose what yesterday prevented only after a present observation corrects or adds to the old count. |
+| 52:117 | “It was a case. He had taken it…” | `books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:45` — “He knew it as an object before he knew it as a fact…” | split | Across three manuscript paragraphs, the object resolves as an open case with its lid thrown back, its contents outside it in a row, and their order interpreted as the order of taking out rather than packing away. The open kit and its contents are required; the source-shaped **open case → contents out → removal order contrasted with packing order → interrupted work** inference is not. Begin on the cord already running east or on the unfinished ledger, let those tools establish that work stopped, and identify the open case only afterward; alternatively have Seln inventory functions rather than reconstruct the surveyor's packing sequence. |
+
+## Dense-scene rerouting
+
+### Chapter 52, scene 2 (lines 62–104)
+
+Enter on the bolt at the thirty-first step or on Teague's lantern showing dust at the chamber threshold. Let somebody notice a flame behaving wrongly before anyone names the twentieth step, and defer the cold or missing earth-smells until they are inside. Once in the chamber, have Cael begin with an actual measurement or discrepancy—dust, wall courses, ceiling clearance—instead of announcing that this is the proper catalogue and contrasting it with yesterday. The required two lamps, twentieth-step air change, chamber dimensions, carved walls, and Oryn/Karis exchange all remain, but the scene no longer repeats either source sentence's entrance and explanatory order.
+
+Chapter 52 scene 3 needs only the local reorder described in the table; it does not require wholesale restructuring.

